<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre Poivre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1719">1719</a>–<a href="/years/1786">1786</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre Poivre was a French horticulturist and administrator who served as intendant of the Isle de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> from 1767 to 1772. He smuggled clove and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> plants out of the Dutch-controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a> to break the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> spice monopoly, and developed the Pamplemousses garden near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-louis">Port Louis</a> into a centre for acclimatising tropical plants.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-poivre.jpg" alt="Pierre Poivre" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grognard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre_Poivre_1783.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Poivre was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> on 23 August 1719 into the family of a wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> merchant. He joined a Jansenist mission at sixteen and the Society of Foreign Missions four years later, which sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> in 1740, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a>. On 25 January 1745 his ship, the Dauphin, was attacked by the English in the Straits of Banca, and a cannonball wound cost him part of his right arm. After time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a> and Ile de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> he reached France in June 1748 and gave up missionary life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sent as an envoy to Cochinchina, he obtained the right to set up a trading post from King Vo Vuong in 1749 and then raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> territories for plants; five of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> plants reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> in December 1753. A voyage of 1754–1755 in search of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a> took him towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a>, and he brought back <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and breadfruit. After capture by the English he retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in 1757 and wrote Voyages d'un Philosophe, published in 1769 and read by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>.</p><p>The Duc de Praslin offered him the post of intendant of Ile de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> in 1766, and he sailed in 1767 with his wife, Françoise Robin. He made the islands self-sufficient in food, improved the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-louis">Port Louis</a>, protected forests against erosion and employed Philibert Commerson to lay out the garden of Pamplemousses. He limited slaves' working hours but also brought in measures against runaways and inter-racial marriages, and the number of slaves rose from 15,000 in 1767 to 49,000 in 1797.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Poivre resigned in 1772 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where a royal pension was decided in 1775; he died at his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>. His widow later married Pierre Samuel du Pont de Nemours, who wrote his biography. The Pamplemousses garden, developed further by Jean-Nicolas Céré, is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sir-seewoosagur-ramgoolam-botanical-garden">Sir Seewoosagur Ramgoolam Botanical Garden</a>, and the Poivre Islands atoll of the Amirante Islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a> is named after him. Some authors see his name, which means <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, behind the Peter Piper rhyme.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pierre Poivre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The intendant <b>Pierre Poivre</b> introduced spices and created the Pamplemousses garden.</blockquote><a href="/state/isle-de-france">Isle de France</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.77,4.84&amp;year=1762" aria-label="Lyon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4664 551 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pierre Poivre"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5114 821h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5114 821h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.77,4.84&amp;year=1762">What was here in 1762? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
